 <!-- CARROT:MANUAL:BEGIN GapSafeDistance -->
 <!-- CARROT:REVIEW reviewed-for="" -->
+<!-- CARROT:SECTION:BEGIN BEHAVIOR -->
+### Behavior
+
+When on, a gap level picks how hard the lead may brake at most instead of a time gap, and the minimum gap from which ego can still stop under that assumption becomes the danger floor. The following target is 0.15 s × speed beyond the minimum gap.
+
+- **On:** The minimum gap is reaction delay × speed + speed²/8 − lead speed²/(2 × assumed decel) + stop distance. It applies only where a lead obstacle binds the plan; cruise targets and traffic stops keep the TF values. The gap grows faster with speed than now.
+- **Off:** Nothing is computed; behavior is unchanged.
+- **Changes:** Turning it on while driving blends in over 3 s, and a level change that lengthens the gap takes up to 3 s. Shortening the gap or turning it off applies at once.
+<!-- CARROT:SECTION:END BEHAVIOR -->
+
+<!-- CARROT:SECTION:BEGIN USAGE -->
+### Usage
+
+Turn it on when the gap to the lead feels short at high speed. Adjust the per-level assumption with GapSafeLeadDecel1-4 and the reaction delay with GapSafeReaction; changes apply within about 5 s without restarting.
+<!-- CARROT:SECTION:END USAGE -->
+
+<!-- CARROT:SECTION:BEGIN REFERENCE -->
+### Reference
+
+<!-- CARROT:NOTICE level="WARNING" -->
+> [!WARNING]
+> Experimental and not validated on a vehicle. In log replay of 15 stopped-lead approaches with only this setting on, the median peak deceleration grew at level 1 (−2.12 → −2.34 m/s²). At level 4 the minimum gap in 30–50 km/h hard-braking synthetic cases was 0.1–0.7 m shorter than the current level 4.
+<!-- CARROT:NOTICE:END -->
+<!-- CARROT:SECTION:END REFERENCE -->
 <!-- CARROT:MANUAL:END GapSafeDistance -->
 
 <!-- CARROT:SETTING:END GapSafeDistance -->
